WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.840
- [Narrator] For years, the cornerstone

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:06.289
of the holiday shopping
season has been Black Friday.

00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:07.160
(crowd screaming)

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.410
- [Reporter] In Buffalo, New York,

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:10.950
shoppers stampeded at Target.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.980
- And the crowds just keep
getting bigger and bigger.

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.970
This place is packed with people

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:18.690
and it has been since five o'clock.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.500
- Not only is it a time of sales,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.830
but it helps people psychologically,

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:23.937
helps those shoppers think,

00:00:23.937 --> 00:00:27.130
"Oh yeah, Christmas is
coming, Hanukkah is coming.

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.210
I'm gonna be with my family.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.070
Let me start buying things."

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.540
- [Narrator] Waking up
at the crack of dawn

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.470
to rush into stores became somewhat

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.560
of a holiday tradition for many families.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.185
- These are deadly discounts!

00:00:38.185 --> 00:00:39.510
(indistinguishable shouting)

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.670
- [Narrator] But this year is
shaping up to look different.

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:45.200
- What the pandemic has
done is actually accelerate

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.290
the trends that we'd seen.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.370
And that is a move to contactless,
a move to convenience,

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.370
really a move toward digital

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:55.950
instead of traditional
shopping experience.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.050
So I think that's gonna
be a lasting change

00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.250
that we see as a result.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.020
- [Narrator] The shopping
tradition dates back

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.990
to the 1920s and 30s
when families would start

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:07.650
buying holiday gifts the
day after Thanksgiving.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.210
Traditionally, this was the
first day that retailers

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.440
would start advertising for Christmas.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.810
But by the 2000s,
companies were announcing

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:18.220
Black Friday door buster
deals earlier and earlier.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.130
(crowd screams)

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.330
- [Reporter] Target stores
opened at five this morning

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:25.000
and there were plenty of
excited shoppers on hand.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.260
- So there were these few horrific moments

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:31.160
where people were dying or
getting seriously injured

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.700
because of people rushing into a building.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.180
- [Narrator] Some department
stores were opening at midnight

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:37.710
and within a few years,

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.600
the deals were creeping
into Thanksgiving day.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.340
By the 2010s, the one
day event had ballooned

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:45.900
to cover the full holiday weekend.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.520
But around this time,
retailers were also launching

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.810
more online sales on the
Monday after Thanksgiving.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.440
This was known as Cyber Monday
and it was growing fast.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.690
In 2016, Cyber Monday
sales hit a new record

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.580
of nearly three and a
half billion dollars.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.220
Digital became much more
important to the consumer.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:06.850
So what we found was Cyber
Monday actually started

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:08.950
to take on a heightened importance.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.440
- [Narrator] For many
companies, thanksgiving weekend,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.750
starting with Black Friday and
stretching into Cyber Monday

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.660
is one of the most
lucrative times of the year.

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.550
But recently appetite for
the chaos of Black Friday

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.350
has started to fade.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.140
And then in 2020, the retail industry

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.430
and how people shopped changed.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:30.570
- Retailers across the
country announcing closures

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:31.830
and shortening their store hours

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.380
in the hopes of limiting the outbreak.

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.190
- Add in the coronavirus pandemic

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.670
and there are retailers
that may not survive.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.230
- We've seen massive
amounts of retail closings,

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:43.600
accelerated bankruptcies this year.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.480
That means there's probably
gonna be fewer stores

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:50.850
and there might be more of a consolidation

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:53.810
in where people go to some
of these big box stores

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.930
that have been open and
operating the whole time.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.350
- [Narrator] During lock
downs in March and April,

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.110
spending slid off a cliff,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.110
with a record drop in retail
sales as people were forced

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.710
to stay home due to the
coronavirus pandemic.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:09.510
Between April and October,
around five thousand US stores

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:10.940
went out of business.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.490
But even the retailers that
had weathered the pandemic

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.330
are changing the way they operate.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:19.680
- This year, retailers for the
first time in modern memory,

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.230
are saying we're not gonna
be open on Thanksgiving.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.140
Black Friday, we'll be
open the day afterwards,

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.550
but on Thanksgiving, deals are online.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:30.010
- [Narrator] When stores
open, consumers can expect

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:33.380
limited hours and
capacity, curbside pickups,

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.290
and a much calmer
atmosphere than years past.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.450
- When folks go in a store,
they're wearing a mask,

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.501
they're being counted,

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:41.900
and stores are being cleaned all the time.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.270
- [Narrator] But perhaps the
biggest change of them all,

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.260
the shift to online shopping.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.160
A recent survey found
that 70% of consumers

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.230
plan to shop online more than normal

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.230
to avoid crowds and
exposure to the coronavirus.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:56.450
But this doesn't mean that
brick and mortar stores

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:58.100
will disappear entirely.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.320
- We mostly still shop
in stores, even now.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:02.320
Even in a pandemic.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.760
I know folks have been talking for years

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.770
about the fact that stores
would just slowly disappear.

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:09.790
Shopping will change, how we
discover products will change.

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.780
- [Narrator] And how these
products get delivered

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.210
will change too.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:15.750
As a result of the shift
to online shopping,

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.760
eCommerce stores are facing
major delivery logistics

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.540
and shopping hurdles this year.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.230
During the beginning of the pandemic,

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:24.500
consumers were faced
with month-long delays

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:27.970
as ports closed and
supply chains broke down.

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.980
Now the question is
whether shipping companies

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.450
can handle the mass
influx of online orders

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.843
that are expected over
the next two months.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.610
- A big reason that
retailers are pushing people

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.880
to shop online early
is to spread out sales

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.670
and give a little breathing room

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.330
to the eCommerce supply chains.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:45.163
- [Narrator] But right now,

00:04:45.163 --> 00:04:47.230
the numbers don't look too promising.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:48.990
The shipping industry is expected to be

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.790
more than four million
packages over capacity

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.280
during the peak of the
holiday season this year.

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.100
- It's gonna be really
hard to order something

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.930
two days before Christmas
and have it arrive.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.820
That literally might not be an option

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.510
even if you're willing to
pay 50 bucks for shipping.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:05.130
Because there won't be
capacity to get it to you.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.720
Which could push people to stores.

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.060
- [Narrator] But at the same time,

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.970
many have lost their jobs
due to the pandemic this year

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.433
and may not have the ability to buy gifts.

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:19.310
- We have around 20 million
people on unemployment.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.010
There was a little bit of
a false sense of security

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:25.040
that came because we got big
stimulus checks in the spring.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.410
There was additional unemployment
that people were getting

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.560
until the end of July.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.870
A lot of that is gone or up in the air

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:34.490
and hasn't really been resolved.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:39.490
So, retailers are definitely
ready for lots of consumers

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:43.000
not to feel like buying at
all or buying a lot less.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.910
And even for folks who have jobs,

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:49.130
I think my sense is talking
to retailers and consultants,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.620
those folks are saving a lot more,

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.030
which points to a little bit of caution.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:55.750
- [Narrator] This means
that certain retailers

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:57.440
that cater to pandemic living,

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.340
like groceries and home improvement,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:01.410
could continue to see profits

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.480
while those that would
traditionally see big sales

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.160
around Black Friday, such
as clothing and toys,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.690
could see bigger losses.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.220
The past year has upended
the retail industry

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:11.970
and shaken up what is traditionally

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.340
the biggest sales period of the year,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.900
but most notably, 2020 has solidified

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.760
the trend towards online
shopping as a permanent part

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.593
of the retail industries future.

